window.RAD_DOCS = {
  "01_what_is_rad.md": "# Chapter 1: What is Rad?\n\n## 1.1 Philosophy & Design Goals\n\nThe **Rad programming language** is a modern, statically typed systems programming language designed for writing high-performance, predictable, and robust native software. Rad was created to offer the mechanical sympathy, raw performance, and low-level hardware control of C, paired with the safety, ergonomics, and clarity of modern languages\u2014without requiring a heavy runtime garbage collector or overly complex compile-time lifetime annotations.\n\n### Core Tenets\n\n1. **Self-Contained Static Executables**: Every binary produced by the Rad compiler (`radc`) is linked as a static Position-Independent Executable (`static-pie`) against a canonical Musl libc sysroot. Executables have zero dynamic library dependencies (`no PT_INTERP`, `no DT_NEEDED`), running reliably across any Linux kernel without distribution-specific runtime issues or glibc version mismatches.\n2. **Embedded In-Process LLVM & LLD Engine**: Starting in version 0.2.0, `radc` embeds the LLVM code generation engine and LLD ELF linker directly in-process via a high-performance C++ backend bridge. This eliminates the latency and file I/O overhead of spawning external assembler and linker subprocesses (`rad-llc`, `rad-lld`), enabling near-instant compilation directly from source to optimized native machine code.\n3. **Zero-Overhead C & C++ Interoperability**: Rad speaks the C ABI natively. Standard C library functions (`malloc`, `free`, `puts`, `printf`, `system`, POSIX file I/O) are direct zero-cost calls without wrapper bloat, marshaling, or FFI barriers. Multi-language projects compile smoothly using bundled `rad-clang` and `rad-clang++`.\n4. **Pure Native Standard Library & Syscalls**: Rad's standard library (`std`) provides rich subsystems\u2014including raw terminal manipulation, ANSI color styling, Kitty keyboard protocol event parsing, double-buffered console rendering, and nanosecond monotonic timing\u2014implemented purely in Rad using direct compiler system-call intrinsics, completely independent of libc wrappers, ncurses, or external C libraries.\n5. **Predictable Memory Semantics**: Rad uses explicit value semantics, direct heap allocations via built-in dynamic collections (`String`, `Vec<T>`), and transparent raw pointer manipulation (`*raw T`) when interfacing with hardware or legacy systems. There is no hidden runtime overhead, tracing garbage collection pauses, or hidden stop-the-world phases.\n6. **Self-Hosted, Single-Pass/Two-Pass Speed**: The production Rad compiler (`radc`) is written entirely in Rad. It compiles directly into SSA (Static Single Assignment) LLVM IR, pairing blazing compile times with LLVM's world-class optimization pipeline (`-O2`, `-O3`).\n7. **Batteries-Included Unified Toolchain**: Rad ships as a complete, hermetic toolchain comprising the compiler (`radc`), the package manager and build orchestrator (`rad`), the embedded LLVM/LLD engine, and full C/C++ compilers (`rad-clang`, `rad-clang++`).\n\n---\n\n## 1.2 Architectural Overview\n\nThe Rad ecosystem is structured into four tightly integrated layers:\n\n```\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502                 Rad Developer Applications                  \u2502\n\u2502       src/*.rad, rad.toml, packages, terminal games         \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                               \u2502\n                               \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502               Rad Toolchain & Package Manager               \u2502\n\u2502                 rad (Package Manager & CLI)                 \u2502\n\u2502   \u2022 rad new / rad init            \u2022 rad run / rad test      \u2502\n\u2502   \u2022 rad build [--release]         \u2022 rad doctor / rad check  \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                               \u2502\n                               \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502            radc (Self-Hosted Compiler & Backend)            \u2502\n\u2502   \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510   \u2502\n\u2502   \u2502  Frontend: Lexer, Pratt AST Parser, SSA Generator   \u2502   \u2502\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518   \u2502\n\u2502                              \u25bc                              \u2502\n\u2502   \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510   \u2502\n\u2502   \u2502  Embedded Backend Bridge (LLVM & LLD In-Process)    \u2502   \u2502\n\u2502   \u2502  \u2022 LLVM TargetMachine: In-memory IR to Object (.o)  \u2502   \u2502\n\u2502   \u2502  \u2022 LLD ELF Driver: Direct in-process static linking \u2502   \u2502\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518   \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                               \u2502\n                               \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502              Canonical Musl Target Sysroot                  \u2502\n\u2502   Target: x86_64-unknown-linux-musl                         \u2502\n\u2502   Assets: rcrt1.o, crti.o, libc.a, libclang_rt.builtins     \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                               \u2502\n                               \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502                 Standalone Static PIE Binary                \u2502\n\u2502             Zero shared library dependencies                \u2502\n\u2502         Runs anywhere on Linux: Alpine, Ubuntu, Arch        \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n\n### The Component Pipeline\n\n- **`rad`**: The user-facing command-line interface and package orchestrator. It manages project layout, initializes packages (`rad new`, `rad init`), coordinates compilation modes (`rad build`), runs test suites (`rad test`), and performs comprehensive toolchain diagnostics (`rad doctor`).\n- **`radc`**: The self-hosted compiler. Written in pure Rad, it tokenizes source files with a high-speed lexer, constructs an arena-based Abstract Syntax Tree using a Pratt recursive descent parser, validates types and scopes, and directly generates optimized textual SSA LLVM IR.\n- **Embedded LLVM & LLD Engine**: Rather than invoking external command-line tools across multiple disk read/write cycles, `radc` links directly with LLVM's TargetMachine C API and LLD's `lld::elf::link` entry point. Intermediate bitcode is compiled to object machine code in memory, and the executable is linked into a static PIE binary in a single unified compiler invocation.\n- **`rad-clang` & `rad-clang++`**: Hermetic C and C++ compiler launchers configured with Rad's static Musl sysroot, enabling seamless mixed-language projects and native C/C++ static library compilation.\n\n---\n\n## 1.3 Practical Code Examples\n\nTo get a concrete sense of Rad's syntax and ergonomics, review the following real-world examples:\n\n### Example 1: Hello World & Dynamic Collections\n\n```rad\n// main.rad \u2014 Basic syntax, String, and Vec dynamic collections\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let greeting = \"Hello from Rad!\"\n    println(greeting)\n\n    // Dynamic contiguous vector\n    var numbers: Vec<i32> = Vec::new()\n    var i = 1\n    while i <= 5 {\n        numbers.push(i * 10)\n        i = i + 1\n    }\n\n    print(\"Collected numbers: \")\n    var j = 0usize\n    while j < numbers.len {\n        print(numbers.get(j))\n        print(\" \")\n        j = j + 1\n    }\n    println(\"\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Example 2: Structs, Methods & Value Semantics\n\n```rad\n// Point struct with methods and value transformations\nstruct Point {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\n\nimpl Point {\n    fn new(x: i32, y: i32) -> Point {\n        return Point { x: x, y: y }\n    }\n\n    fn translate(var self, dx: i32, dy: i32) {\n        self.x = self.x + dx\n        self.y = self.y + dy\n    }\n\n    fn distance_squared(self, other: Point) -> i32 {\n        let dx = self.x - other.x\n        let dy = self.y - other.y\n        return dx * dx + dy * dy\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var p1 = Point::new(10, 20)\n    let p2 = Point::new(13, 24)\n\n    p1.translate(3, 4)\n    println(\"Distance squared: \")\n    println(p1.distance_squared(p2))\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Example 3: Zero-Overhead C ABI Interoperability\n\nCalling standard C library functions or POSIX system APIs requires zero wrappers or marshaling code:\n\n```rad\n// Direct C ABI declarations\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn puts(s: str) -> i32;\n    fn getpid() -> i32;\n    fn malloc(size: usize) -> *raw u8;\n    fn free(ptr: *raw u8) -> void;\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    puts(\"Direct call to C puts() with zero overhead!\")\n    let pid = getpid()\n    println(pid)\n\n    // Raw memory allocation\n    let buf = malloc(1024usize)\n    if (buf as usize) != 0usize {\n        buf[0] = 65u8 // 'A'\n        free(buf)\n    }\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Example 4: Pure-Rad System Call & Monotonic Timing\n\nRad standard library features do not require libc wrappers:\n\n```rad\nimport std::time\nimport std::terminal\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    // High-resolution monotonic timing via Linux clock_gettime syscall intrinsic\n    let start = time::now_ms()\n\n    // Pure native ANSI terminal styling\n    terminal::enable_raw_mode()\n    terminal::set_fg(terminal::Color::Cyan)\n    print(\"Welcome to native Rad systems programming!\\n\")\n    terminal::reset_color()\n\n    time::sleep_ms(250) // Microsecond-accurate nanosleep syscall\n\n    let elapsed = time::now_ms() - start\n    terminal::disable_raw_mode()\n\n    println(\"Elapsed ms: \")\n    println(elapsed)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Statement Separation Rules\n\nRad recognizes both newlines and semicolons as statement separators:\n- **Newline-separated statements**: Statements placed on separate lines do not require terminating semicolons:\n  ```rad\n  println(\"Hello\")\n  println(\"World\")\n\n  let x = 10\n  let y = 20\n  ```\n- **Same-line statements**: Multiple statements placed on the same line must be explicitly separated by semicolons:\n  ```rad\n  println(\"Hello\"); println(\"World\")\n  let x = 10; let y = 20\n  ```\n- **Same-line rejection without separators**: Adjacent statements on the same line without a semicolon are rejected at compile time with a clear syntax error (`error[E0001]`):\n  ```rad\n  println(\"Hello\") println(\"World\") // Error: expected ';' or newline between statements on the same line\n  ```\n- **Optional trailing semicolons & empty statements**: Trailing semicolons before closing braces (`let x = 10; }`) and consecutive semicolons (`x = 1;; y = 2;`) representing empty statements are fully accepted.\n- **Multiline expressions**: Expressions spanning across lines (parentheses, binary operators, method chains) preserve their natural continuation without requiring semicolons.\n- **Struct fields**: Fields can be separated by commas (`,`), semicolons (`;`), or newlines. Same-line struct fields require an explicit separator (`struct Point { x: i32, y: i32 }` or `struct Point { x: i32; y: i32 }`).\n\n---\n\n## 1.4 How Rad Compares to Other Languages\n\n| Feature | Rad | C | C++ | Rust | Zig | Go |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Garbage Collection** | **None** | None | None | None | None | Concurrent Tracing GC |\n| **Runtime Overhead** | **Zero** | Zero | Minimal | Zero | Zero | Moderate (Scheduler, GC) |\n| **Default Binary Format** | **Static PIE** | Dynamic ELF | Dynamic ELF | Dynamic / Static | Static PIE | Static ELF |\n| **Compiler Backend** | **Embedded LLVM+LLD** | External | External | External LLVM | Bundled / LLVM | Custom / Internal |\n| **C Interoperability** | **Direct C ABI** | Native | Native | Via FFI bindings | Direct | Via Cgo (High Overhead) |\n| **Package Manager** | **Built-in (`rad`)** | None | CMake/Conan | Built-in (`cargo`) | Built-in | Built-in (`go`) |\n| **Type Safety** | **Strong, Static** | Weak | Strong | Strict Lifetime Borrowing | Strong | Strong |\n| **Compilation Speed** | **Fast (In-Process)** | Very Fast | Slow | Slow to Moderate | Fast | Very Fast |\n| **Standard Library** | **Pure Native (Syscall)**| Libc | Libc/Libstdc++| Libc / Core | Syscall | Syscall |\n| **Toolchain Self-Containment** | **Full (Sysroot Included)** | External | External | External Linkers | Self-Contained | Self-Contained |\n\n---\n\n## 1.5 When to Choose Rad\n\nChoose Rad when your project demands:\n- **Zero-Dependency Portability**: Standalone binaries that deploy effortlessly to any Linux environment (minimal containers, Alpine, Debian, Arch, embedded appliances) without shared library conflicts or libc incompatibility.\n- **Embedded Compiler Performance**: Rapid build cycles powered by in-process LLVM code generation and LLD static linking without subprocess overhead.\n- **Low-Level Control with High Ergonomics**: Explicit pointer management, predictable value semantics, dynamic vectors, strings, and pattern matching without GC pauses or complex lifetime puzzles.\n- **Pure Native Systems Development**: Terminal user interfaces, arcade engines, game loops, system daemons, and operating system utilities built directly against kernel syscalls.\n- **Frictionless C/C++ Integration**: Zero-cost calling into existing C/C++ libraries and header ecosystems without complicated binding generators.\n",
  "02_installation.md": "# Chapter 2: Installation & Toolchain Setup\n\n## 2.1 Quick Installation (Automated)\n\nThe quickest way to install the complete, production-ready Rad toolchain on Linux is using the official installer script via `curl`:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GamerxIOEPB/rad-lang/main/scripts/install.sh | sh\n```\n\n### What the Installer Does:\n1. Detects your host architecture (`x86_64`) and operating system (`Linux`).\n2. Downloads the official release archive (`rad-0.2.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz`) from GitHub Releases.\n3. Verifies the cryptographic SHA-256 integrity hash against `SHA256SUMS`.\n4. Extracts all binaries, libraries, standard library modules, target sysroots, and embedded LLVM runtime components to `~/.rad`.\n5. Automatically configures your shell environment (`~/.bashrc`, `~/.zshrc`, or `~/.profile`) to ensure `~/.rad/bin` is exported in your `PATH`.\n\nTo activate your installation in the current terminal, source your shell profile or open a new terminal session:\n\n```bash\nsource ~/.bashrc\nrad --version\nradc --version\n```\n\nExpected output:\n```text\nrad 0.2.0 (x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)\nradc 0.2.0 (self-hosted x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.2 Verifying with `rad doctor`\n\nRad provides an integrated diagnostic doctor command that verifies every component of your installed toolchain:\n\n```bash\nrad doctor\n```\n\nExpected diagnostic output:\n```text\nRad Toolchain Doctor\n====================\n\n  [OK] radc compiler: found (/home/username/.rad/bin/radc)\n  [OK] Embedded LLVM & LLD backend: integrated in-process\n  [OK] Standard library sources: verified at /home/username/.rad/lib/rad\n  [OK] Target sysroot (x86_64-unknown-linux-musl): verified at /home/username/.rad/lib/targets/x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\n\nStatus: Toolchain is healthy and ready for self-contained native compilation.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.3 Non-Interactive & Custom Prefix Installation\n\nFor continuous integration (CI) workflows, Docker containers, or system-wide deployments, you can pass arguments to the installer:\n\n```bash\n# Non-interactive install without shell prompts:\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GamerxIOEPB/rad-lang/main/scripts/install.sh | sh -s -- -y\n\n# Install to a custom directory prefix (e.g., /opt/rad):\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GamerxIOEPB/rad-lang/main/scripts/install.sh | sh -s -- --prefix /opt/rad -y\n\n# Install without modifying shell configuration files:\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GamerxIOEPB/rad-lang/main/scripts/install.sh | sh -s -- --no-modify-path -y\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.4 Building from Source\n\nIf you prefer building the toolchain directly from source:\n\n### Prerequisites:\n- Git\n- GNU Make (`make`)\n- Host Clang & LLVM development libraries (LLVM 18+ or 23+)\n- Existing `radc` compiler or prebuilt bootstrap\n\n### Build Steps:\n```bash\n# Clone the official repository\ngit clone https://github.com/GamerxIOEPB/rad-lang.git\ncd rad-lang\n\n# Build all binaries (compiler with embedded backend, package manager, launchers)\nmake all\n\n# Verify the freshly built binaries in bin/\n./bin/radc --version\n./bin/rad doctor\n\n# Run regression test suite (152 tests)\nbash rad_tests/radtest/runner/run_tests.sh\n\n# Install into default prefix (~/.rad)\nmake install\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.5 Toolchain Directory Layout\n\nWhen installed into `~/.rad`, the directory structure is organized as follows:\n\n```\n~/.rad/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 bin/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 rad           # Rad package manager & build orchestrator\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 radc          # Self-hosted native compiler with embedded LLVM & LLD\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 rad-clang     # Static PIE C compiler launcher\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 rad-clang++   # Static PIE C++ compiler launcher\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 lib/\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 llvm/         # Embedded LLVM runtime libraries and headers\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 clang/        # Clang resource headers (stddef.h, stdarg.h, etc.)\n\u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 rad/          # Rad standard library sources\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 core/     # Fundamental types, options, results, math\n\u2502   \u2502   \u251c\u2500\u2500 alloc/    # Dynamic collections (String, Vec)\n\u2502   \u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 std/      # Terminal, keyboard, console, time, io, fs, process\n\u2502   \u2514\u2500\u2500 targets/\n\u2502       \u2514\u2500\u2500 x86_64-unknown-linux-musl/\n\u2502           \u251c\u2500\u2500 crt/  # C runtime startup objects (rcrt1.o, crti.o, crtn.o)\n\u2502           \u251c\u2500\u2500 include/ # C headers\n\u2502           \u2514\u2500\u2500 lib/  # Static libraries (libc.a, libclang_rt.builtins-x86_64.a)\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 share/\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 rad/          # License manifests, release metadata, and targets.toml\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2.6 Environment Variables\n\nThe toolchain respects the following environment variables:\n\n- **`RAD_HOME`**: Overrides the installation directory prefix (default: `~/.rad`).\n- **`PATH`**: Ensure `$RAD_HOME/bin` is in your `PATH`.\n- **`RADC`**: Explicit path to the `radc` binary used when executing `make` or build scripts.\n- **`RAD_LIB_DIR`**: Explicit path override for the standard library sources.\n",
  "03_getting_started.md": "# Chapter 3: Getting Started\n\n## 3.1 Your First Rad Program (\"Hello, World!\")\n\nLet's start by writing and compiling a standalone Rad program from scratch.\n\n### 1. Create a Source File\nCreate a new file named `hello.rad`:\n\n```rad\n// hello.rad \u2014 My first Rad program\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let message: str = \"Hello, World from Rad!\"\n    let year: i32 = 2026\n\n    println(message)\n    print(\"Welcome to Rad in year: \")\n    println(year)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Compile with `radc`\nInvoke the self-hosted Rad compiler directly:\n\n```bash\nradc hello.rad -o hello\n```\n\nThe compiler directly emits optimized machine code and links the static PIE executable in-process.\n\n### 3. Run the Native Executable\n```bash\n./hello\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nHello, World from Rad!\nWelcome to Rad in year: 2026\n```\n\n### 4. Inspect the Executable\nCheck the properties of the resulting binary using standard Linux utilities:\n\n```bash\nfile hello\nldd hello\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nhello: ELF 64-bit LSB pie executable, x86-64, version 1 (SYSV), statically linked, stripped\nnot a dynamic executable\n```\n\nNotice that `hello` is **statically linked** and **not a dynamic executable**! It has zero dependencies on host glibc versions and will run identically on Ubuntu, Debian, Alpine Linux, Arch Linux, or container environments.\n\n---\n\n## 3.2 Multi-File Projects & Modular Imports\n\nRad supports modular development across multiple files using intuitive file-relative imports.\n\n### 1. Create a Helper Module (`math_ops.rad`)\n```rad\n// math_ops.rad \u2014 Reusable math utilities\npub fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\n    return a + b\n}\n\npub fn factorial(n: i32) -> i32 {\n    if n <= 1 {\n        return 1\n    }\n    return n * factorial(n - 1)\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Import into `main.rad`\nUse the `./` syntax to import local sibling files:\n\n```rad\n// main.rad \u2014 Modular application entry point\nimport ./math_ops.rad\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let sum = math_ops::add(15, 27)\n    let fact = math_ops::factorial(5)\n\n    print(\"Sum: \")\n    println(sum)\n\n    print(\"Factorial of 5: \")\n    println(fact)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Compile Directly\n```bash\nradc main.rad -o math_app\n./math_app\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nSum: 42\nFactorial of 5: 120\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3.3 Using the Rad Package Manager (`rad`)\n\nFor production projects and libraries, use the `rad` package manager to initialize, build, and organize your software.\n\n### Creating a New Application Package\nRun `rad new` followed by the project name:\n\n```bash\nrad new my_project\ncd my_project\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nCreating new Rad package...\nCreated binary (application) package successfully!\n```\n\n### Project Directory Layout\n`rad new` creates a standard project layout:\n\n```\nmy_project/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 rad.toml        # Package manifest & configuration\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 .gitignore      # Ignores target/ build output and temporary files\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 src/\n    \u2514\u2500\u2500 main.rad    # Main application entry point\n```\n\n### The Manifest (`rad.toml`)\nThe `rad.toml` file defines package metadata:\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"my_project\"\nversion = \"0.2.0\"\nedition = \"2026\"\n\n[dependencies]\n# External dependencies\n```\n\n### The Generated Entry Point (`src/main.rad`)\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    println(\"Hello from Rad Package!\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3.4 Building and Running Packages\n\n### Checking Syntax without Compiling\nTo rapidly check your code for syntax errors and structural validity without invoking backend code generation:\n\n```bash\nrad check\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nChecking Rad package syntax and types...\nCheck complete: 0 errors.\nCheck passed: 0 errors.\n```\n\n### Compiling with `rad build`\nCompile the package into a debug executable:\n\n```bash\nrad build\n```\n\nThis compiles `src/main.rad` into `target/debug/main`.\n\nTo build an optimized release binary with LLVM `-O3` optimizations:\n\n```bash\nrad build --release\n```\n\nThis places the optimized binary in `target/release/main`.\n\n### Running with `rad run`\nBuild and immediately execute your application in one step:\n\n```bash\nrad run\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nCompiling project with radc...\nFinished build successfully.\nRunning...\nHello from Rad Package!\n```\n\nPass command-line arguments to your application by separating them with `--`:\n\n```bash\nrad run -- --port 8080 --verbose\n```\n\n### Cleaning Build Artifacts\nTo wipe generated binaries and object files:\n\n```bash\nrad clean\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3.5 Creating a Library Package\n\nIf you are developing a reusable library rather than an executable application, pass the `--lib` flag:\n\n```bash\nrad new my_library --lib\n```\n\nThis creates a library skeleton with `src/lib.rad`:\n\n```rad\n// src/lib.rad \u2014 Reusable library interface\npub fn compute_hash(seed: u64, input: str) -> u64 {\n    var hash = seed\n    let len = str_len(input)\n    var i = 0usize\n    while i < len {\n        hash = (hash ^ (input[i] as u64)) * 1099511628211u64\n        i = i + 1\n    }\n    return hash\n}\n```\n",
  "04_variables_and_types.md": "# Chapter 4: Variables, Mutability & Types\n\n## 4.1 Variables & Mutability\n\nIn Rad, variables are declared using either `let` or `var`.\n\n### Immutable Variables (`let`)\nBy default, variables introduced with `let` are immutable. Once initialized, their value cannot be changed:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let x: i64 = 42\n    // x = 50  <-- Compile error: assignment to immutable variable 'x'\n    print(\"x is: \")\n    println(x)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Mutable Variables (`var`)\nWhen a variable needs to be reassigned or mutated, declare it using `var`:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var count: i64 = 0\n    count = count + 1\n    count = count * 2\n    print(\"count is: \")\n    println(count)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Type Inference\nRad infers the type of a variable from its initializer expression when the type annotation is omitted:\n\n```rad\nlet message = \"Hello from Rad\" // inferred as str\nlet active = true             // inferred as bool\nvar counter = 100i64          // inferred as i64 from suffix\nlet ratio = 3.14159           // inferred as f64\n```\n\nExplicit type annotations (`name: Type`) are encouraged for public module APIs and critical interfaces.\n\n---\n\n## 4.2 Primitive Data Types\n\nRad provides an explicit, machine-level set of scalar data types.\n\n### 1. Integer Types\nRad provides signed and unsigned integers across standard bit widths:\n\n| Type | Signed | Bit Width | Description / Range | Literal Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `i8` | Yes | 8 | -128 to 127 | `10i8`, `-5i8` |\n| `i16` | Yes | 16 | -32,768 to 32,767 | `1000i16` |\n| `i32` | Yes | 32 | -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647 (default integer) | `42`, `42i32` |\n| `i64` | Yes | 64 | -9,223,372,036,854,775,808 to 9,223,372,036,854,775,807 | `100i64` |\n| `isize` | Yes | Pointer width | Signed pointer width (64-bit on x86_64) | `5isize` |\n| `u8` | No | 8 | 0 to 255 (standard byte) | `255u8`, `0u8` |\n| `u16` | No | 16 | 0 to 65,535 | `60000u16` |\n| `u32` | No | 32 | 0 to 4,294,967,295 | `100000u32` |\n| `u64` | No | 64 | 0 to 18,446,744,073,709,551,615 | `1000000u64` |\n| `usize` | No | Pointer width | Unsigned pointer width (64-bit on x86_64) | `0usize`, `1024usize` |\n\n### 2. Floating-Point Types\n- `f32`: Single-precision 32-bit IEEE 754 floating point.\n- `f64`: Double-precision 64-bit IEEE 754 floating point.\n\n```rad\nlet pi: f64 = 3.141592653589793\nlet gravity: f32 = 9.81f32\n```\n\n### 3. Boolean Type (`bool`)\nThe `bool` type represents logical truth values:\n- `true`\n- `false`\n\n```rad\nlet is_running: bool = true\nlet is_finished: bool = false\n\nif is_running && !is_finished {\n    println(\"Engine active\")\n}\n```\n\n### 4. String Literals (`str`)\nThe `str` type represents a null-terminated pointer to an immutable sequence of UTF-8 bytes:\n\n```rad\nlet greeting: str = \"Welcome to Rad Systems\"\n```\n\nString literals support standard escape sequences:\n- `\\n`: Newline\n- `\\t`: Tab\n- `\\r`: Carriage return\n- `\\\"`: Double quote\n- `\\\\`: Backslash\n- `\\0`: Null terminator byte\n- `\\x1b`: ANSI escape byte (hex)\n\n---\n\n## 4.3 Explicit Type Casting (`as`)\n\nRad strictly prevents implicit lossy conversions. All conversions between integer sizes, signs, floats, and raw pointers must be explicitly declared using the `as` operator:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let a: i32 = 1000\n    let b: i64 = a as i64        // Widening cast\n    let c: u8  = (a as u8)       // Truncating cast\n    let d: usize = 4096usize\n    let ptr: *raw u8 = d as *raw u8 // Pointer cast\n\n    print(\"b: \")\n    println(b)\n    print(\"c: \")\n    println(c as i32)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4.4 Raw Pointer Types\n\nFor low-level hardware interactions, system calls, and C ABI interop, Rad provides raw pointers:\n\n- `*raw T`: Raw pointer to type `T`.\n- `*raw *raw u8`: Multi-level raw pointer (such as `argv: *raw *raw u8`).\n\n```rad\n// Pointer declaration, array indexing and casting\nlet message: str = \"Systems\"\nlet ptr: *raw u8 = message as *raw u8\nlet first_byte: u8 = ptr[0usize] // 83 ('S')\n\nprint(\"First byte ASCII: \")\nprintln(first_byte as i32)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4.5 Constants (`const`)\n\nConstants are compile-time immutable values evaluated before code generation. They are declared at file scope with the `const` keyword:\n\n```rad\nconst MAX_BUFFER_SIZE: i64 = 4096\nconst DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS: i64 = 5000\nconst PROTOCOL_VERSION: i32 = 1\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    print(\"Max buffer size: \")\n    println(MAX_BUFFER_SIZE)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4.6 Comments\n\nRad supports both single-line and multi-line block comments:\n\n```rad\n// This is a single-line comment\n\n/*\n * This is a multi-line\n * block comment.\n */\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let a = 10 // Inline comment\n    return 0\n}\n```\n",
  "05_operators_and_expressions.md": "# Chapter 5: Operators & Expressions\n\n## 5.1 Arithmetic Operators\n\nRad supports the standard set of binary and unary arithmetic operators:\n\n| Operator | Name | Example | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `+` | Addition | `a + b` | Calculates the sum of `a` and `b` |\n| `-` | Subtraction | `a - b` | Calculates the difference of `b` from `a` |\n| `*` | Multiplication | `a * b` | Calculates the product of `a` and `b` |\n| `/` | Division | `a / b` | Integer or floating-point quotient |\n| `%` | Modulo | `a % b` | Remainder of integer division |\n| `-` | Negation (unary) | `-x` | Negates the numeric operand |\n\n### Example:\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let a: i64 = 25\n    let b: i64 = 4\n\n    let sum  = a + b       // 29\n    let diff = a - b      // 21\n    let prod = a * b      // 100\n    let quot = a / b      // 6\n    let rem  = a % b       // 1\n    let neg  = -a          // -25\n\n    print(\"sum: \")\n    println(sum)\n    print(\"quot: \")\n    println(quot)\n    print(\"rem: \")\n    println(rem)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5.2 Bitwise Operators\n\nFor systems programming, bitmask manipulation, and protocol framing, Rad provides complete bitwise operators:\n\n| Operator | Name | Example | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `&` | Bitwise AND | `a & b` | Bits present in both operands |\n| `\\|` | Bitwise OR | `a \\| b` | Bits present in either operand |\n| `^` | Bitwise XOR | `a ^ b` | Bits present in exactly one operand |\n| `<<` | Left Shift | `a << 2` | Shifts bits left by specified count |\n| `>>` | Right Shift | `a >> 2` | Shifts bits right by specified count |\n\n### Example:\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let flags: u32 = 0b0010_1010u32\n    let mask:  u32 = 0b0000_1111u32\n\n    let masked = flags & mask\n    let combined = flags | 0b0001_0000u32\n    let shifted = flags << 2\n\n    print(\"masked: \")\n    println(masked as i32)\n    print(\"shifted: \")\n    println(shifted as i32)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5.3 Comparison Operators\n\nComparison operators evaluate expressions and return a `bool` (`true` or `false`):\n\n| Operator | Description | Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `==` | Equal to | `a == b` |\n| `!=` | Not equal to | `a != b` |\n| `<` | Less than | `a < b` |\n| `<=` | Less than or equal to | `a <= b` |\n| `>` | Greater than | `a > b` |\n| `>=` | Greater than or equal to | `a >= b` |\n\n### Example:\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let score: i64 = 85\n\n    let is_passing: bool = score >= 50\n    let is_perfect: bool = score == 100\n\n    if is_passing && !is_perfect {\n        println(\"Passed the test!\")\n    }\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5.4 Logical Operators\n\nRad provides standard short-circuiting Boolean logic operators:\n\n| Operator | Name | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `&&` | Logical AND | Returns `true` only if both operands are `true` (short-circuits) |\n| `\\|\\|` | Logical OR | Returns `true` if either operand is `true` (short-circuits) |\n| `!` | Logical NOT | Inverts the boolean operand |\n\n### Example:\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let has_key = true\n    let is_admin = false\n    let is_emergency = true\n\n    if (has_key || is_admin) && !is_emergency {\n        println(\"Standard access granted\")\n    } else if is_emergency {\n        println(\"Emergency override active!\")\n    }\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5.5 Operator Precedence\n\nOperators in Rad are evaluated following standard mathematical and systems programming precedence rules (from highest to lowest binding power):\n\n1. **Postfix**: Member access (`.`), Method call (`.method()`), Function call (`()`), Indexing (`[]`)\n2. **Unary**: Negation (`-`), Logical NOT (`!`), Bitwise NOT (`~`)\n3. **Multiplicative**: `*`, `/`, `%`\n4. **Additive**: `+`, `-`\n5. **Shift**: `<<`, `>>`\n6. **Bitwise AND**: `&`\n7. **Bitwise XOR**: `^`\n8. **Bitwise OR**: `|`\n9. **Relational / Comparison**: `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`\n10. **Equality**: `==`, `!=`\n11. **Logical AND**: `&&`\n12. **Logical OR**: `||`\n13. **Cast**: `as`\n14. **Assignment**: `=`\n\nUse parentheses `( ... )` to explicitly enforce evaluation order when combining complex expressions.\n\n---\n\n## 5.6 Explicit Type Casting (`as`)\n\nRad strictly prevents implicit lossy conversions. Conversions between numeric types, pointers, and memory representations require an explicit `as` cast:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let big: i64 = 1000\n    let small: i32 = big as i32\n\n    let val: u64 = 0xDEADBEEFu64\n    let as_ptr: *raw u8 = val as *raw u8\n    let back_to_num: usize = as_ptr as usize\n\n    print(\"small: \")\n    println(small)\n    print(\"ptr addr: \")\n    println(back_to_num as i64)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Common Cast Patterns:\n- Numeric width changes: `val_i64 as i32`, `byte as i64`\n- Pointers to integer addresses: `ptr as usize`\n- Integer addresses to raw pointers: `addr as *raw u8`\n- Byte pointers to string references: `byte_ptr as str`\n",
  "06_control_flow.md": "# Chapter 6: Control Flow\n\n## 6.1 Conditional Branching (`if`, `else if`, `else`)\n\nRad uses `if` expressions to execute blocks of code based on a Boolean condition:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let temperature: i64 = 28\n\n    if temperature > 35 {\n        println(\"Heat warning!\")\n    } else if temperature > 20 {\n        println(\"Pleasant warm weather.\")\n    } else if temperature > 10 {\n        println(\"Cool weather.\")\n    } else {\n        println(\"Cold weather alert!\")\n    }\n\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Key Rules:\n- The condition expression must strictly evaluate to a `bool`.\n- Parentheses around conditions are optional (`if x > 0` or `if (x > 0)`).\n- The curly braces `{ ... }` around the body and else blocks are **mandatory**, eliminating classic C \"dangling else\" vulnerabilities.\n\n---\n\n## 6.2 While Loops (`while`)\n\nRad provides the `while` loop for repeated execution while a Boolean condition evaluates to `true`:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var counter: i64 = 0\n\n    while counter < 5 {\n        print(\"Iteration: \")\n        println(counter)\n        counter = counter + 1\n    }\n\n    println(\"Loop completed.\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nIteration: 0\nIteration: 1\nIteration: 2\nIteration: 3\nIteration: 4\nLoop completed.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6.3 Loop Control (`break` & `continue`)\n\n### The `break` Statement\n`break` immediately terminates and exits the enclosing loop:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var i: i64 = 0\n\n    while true {\n        if i >= 4 {\n            break // Exits loop when i reaches 4\n        }\n        print(\"i = \")\n        println(i)\n        i = i + 1\n    }\n\n    println(\"Broke out of loop.\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### The `continue` Statement\n`continue` skips the remainder of the current loop iteration and proceeds immediately to the next condition evaluation:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var i: i64 = 0\n\n    while i < 10 {\n        i = i + 1\n        if i % 2 == 0 {\n            continue // Skip even numbers\n        }\n        print(\"Odd number: \")\n        println(i)\n    }\n\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nOdd number: 1\nOdd number: 3\nOdd number: 5\nOdd number: 7\nOdd number: 9\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6.4 Nested Loops & Grid Traversal\n\nNested loops are common for 2D matrix, game board, and console buffer rendering:\n\n```rad\nfn render_grid(width: i32, height: i32) {\n    var y = 0\n    while y < height {\n        var x = 0\n        while x < width {\n            if x == 0 || x == width - 1 || y == 0 || y == height - 1 {\n                print(\"#\")\n            } else {\n                print(\".\")\n            }\n            x = x + 1\n        }\n        println(\"\")\n        y = y + 1\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    render_grid(8, 4)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\n########\n#......#\n#......#\n########\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6.5 Infinite Loops & Game Loops\n\nInteractive game loops, service polling, and event listeners use `while true` with explicit exit conditions:\n\n```rad\nfn run_engine() {\n    var running: bool = true\n    var frame_count: i64 = 0\n\n    while running {\n        frame_count = frame_count + 1\n        if frame_count >= 1000 {\n            running = false\n            break\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6.6 Block Scope & Statement Separation Rules\n\nEvery block `{ ... }` in Rad (function bodies, `if`/`else` branches, `while`/`loop` bodies, `match` arms) enforces explicit statement separation:\n\n1. **Newline separation**: Semicolons are optional between statements located on distinct lines:\n   ```rad\n   let a = 1\n   let b = 2\n   ```\n\n2. **Same-line semicolons**: If two or more statements share the same source line, they must be separated with `;`:\n   ```rad\n   let a = 1; let b = 2\n   if condition { do_something() }; println(\"next\")\n   ```\n\n3. **Rejection of unseparated same-line statements**:\n   ```rad\n   let a = 1 let b = 2 // Syntax Error: expected ';' or newline between statements on the same line\n   ```\n\n4. **Empty statements & trailing separators**:\n   - Consecutive semicolons (`x = 1;; y = 2`) are tolerated as empty statements.\n   - Semicolons before a closing brace `}` are optional (`{ foo(); }` and `{ foo() }` are both valid).\n\n",
  "07_functions.md": "# Chapter 7: Functions & Program Entry\n\n## 7.1 Declaring Functions\n\nFunctions in Rad are declared using the `fn` keyword, followed by the function name, a comma-separated parameter list enclosed in parentheses, an optional return type arrow (`-> Type`), and a mandatory body block enclosed in `{ ... }`:\n\n```rad\nfn add(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {\n    return a + b\n}\n\nfn greet(name: str) {\n    print(\"Hello, \")\n    println(name)\n}\n```\n\nIf a function does not return a value, the `-> Type` annotation can be omitted (implicitly returning `void`).\n\n---\n\n## 7.2 The `return` Statement\n\nIn Rad, functions that produce a result value must explicitly use the `return` statement:\n\n```rad\nfn max_val(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {\n    if a > b {\n        return a\n    } else {\n        return b\n    }\n}\n```\n\nFunctions can also use early returns for defensive guard clauses:\n\n```rad\nfn compute_ratio(numerator: i64, denominator: i64) -> i64 {\n    if denominator == 0 {\n        println(\"Error: division by zero!\")\n        return 0\n    }\n    return numerator / denominator\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7.3 Mutable Parameters (`var`)\n\nFunction arguments are immutable by default within the function body. If a parameter's local copy needs to be mutated, prefix the parameter name with `var`:\n\n```rad\nfn countdown(var count: i64) {\n    while count > 0 {\n        print(\"T-minus: \")\n        println(count)\n        count = count - 1\n    }\n    println(\"Liftoff!\")\n}\n```\n\nNotice that parameters in Rad are passed by value: modifying `var count` modifies the local parameter variable and does not affect the caller's variable.\n\n---\n\n## 7.4 Public Visibility (`pub`)\n\nWhen authoring multi-file projects or reusable libraries, items that should be accessible outside their defining module are marked with `pub`:\n\n```rad\npub fn calculate_hash(data: *raw u8, len: usize) -> u64 {\n    var hash = 14695981039346656037u64\n    var i = 0usize\n    while i < len {\n        hash = (hash ^ (data[i] as u64)) * 1099511628211u64\n        i = i + 1\n    }\n    return hash\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7.5 Recursion\n\nRad functions fully support recursion:\n\n```rad\npub fn fibonacci(n: i64) -> i64 {\n    if n <= 0 {\n        return 0\n    }\n    if n == 1 {\n        return 1\n    }\n    return fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2)\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7.6 The `main` Entry Point\n\nEvery executable Rad program begins execution at the `main` function. Rad supports two valid signatures for `main`:\n\n### 1. Simple Zero-Argument Entry:\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    println(\"Application running\")\n    return 0 // Process exit code 0 (success)\n}\n```\n\nOr omitting the explicit return type:\n```rad\nfn main() {\n    println(\"Exits with code 0 by default\")\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Command-Line Arguments Entry:\nTo receive command-line arguments passed from the operating system shell:\n\n```rad\nfn main(argc: i32, argv: *raw *raw u8) -> i32 {\n    print(\"Program name: \")\n    println(argv[0usize] as str)\n\n    print(\"Argument count: \")\n    println(argc)\n\n    var i: usize = 1usize\n    while i < (argc as usize) {\n        let arg_str: str = argv[i] as str\n        print(\"Arg: \")\n        println(arg_str)\n        i = i + 1usize\n    }\n\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nHere:\n- `argc`: Number of arguments (including program name at index 0).\n- `argv`: Raw pointer to an array of null-terminated C string pointers (`*raw *raw u8`).\n- Returning an integer value sets the Linux process exit status (inspectable via `$?` in bash).\n",
  "08_structs_and_methods.md": "# Chapter 8: Structs, Fields & Methods\n\n## 8.1 Defining Structs\n\nStructs in Rad allow you to group related fields into a custom compound data type:\n\n```rad\nstruct Point {\n    x: i64,\n    y: i64,\n}\n\nstruct Player {\n    id: i64,\n    health: i32,\n    score: i64,\n    active: bool,\n}\n```\n\nEach field declaration consists of `field_name: Type`, separated by commas.\n\n---\n\n## 8.2 Instantiation & Field Initialization\n\nCreate an instance of a struct using struct literal syntax (`StructName { field: value, ... }`):\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let p = Point {\n        x: 10,\n        y: 20,\n    }\n\n    print(\"Point x: \")\n    println(p.x)\n    print(\"Point y: \")\n    println(p.y)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8.3 Accessing and Mutating Fields\n\n### Accessing Fields\nAccess struct fields using the dot operator (`.`):\n\n```rad\nlet px = p.x\nlet py = p.y\n```\n\n### Mutating Fields\nIf a struct variable is declared with `var`, its fields can be mutated directly:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var p = Point { x: 5, y: 12 }\n    p.x = 100\n    p.y = 200\n\n    print(\"Updated x: \")\n    println(p.x)\n    print(\"Updated y: \")\n    println(p.y)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8.4 Implementation Blocks (`impl`) & Methods\n\nMethods in Rad are organized inside `impl` blocks associated with the struct:\n\n```rad\nstruct Rectangle {\n    width: i64,\n    height: i64,\n}\n\nimpl Rectangle {\n    // Factory constructor function\n    fn create(w: i64, h: i64) -> Rectangle {\n        return Rectangle { width: w, height: h }\n    }\n\n    // Read-only method (immutable self)\n    fn area(self) -> i64 {\n        return self.width * self.height\n    }\n\n    fn perimeter(self) -> i64 {\n        return 2 * (self.width + self.height)\n    }\n\n    // Mutating method (declared with 'var self')\n    fn scale(var self, factor: i64) {\n        self.width = self.width * factor\n        self.height = self.height * factor\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Invoking Methods with Dot Notation\n\nOnce declared inside the `impl` block, methods can be invoked cleanly using dot syntax:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var rect = Rectangle::create(10, 5)\n\n    let a = rect.area()\n    let p = rect.perimeter()\n\n    print(\"Area: \")\n    println(a)\n    print(\"Perimeter: \")\n    println(p)\n\n    // Mutate via method call\n    rect.scale(2)\n    print(\"Scaled Area: \")\n    println(rect.area())\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nArea: 50\nPerimeter: 30\nScaled Area: 200\n```\n\n---\n\n## 8.5 Mutating Methods with `var self`\n\nWhen a method modifies the receiver's fields, declare the first parameter as `var self`:\n\n```rad\nstruct Counter {\n    value: i32,\n}\n\nimpl Counter {\n    fn create() -> Counter {\n        return Counter { value: 0 }\n    }\n\n    fn increment(var self) {\n        self.value = self.value + 1\n    }\n\n    fn add(var self, amount: i32) {\n        self.value = self.value + amount\n    }\n\n    fn get(self) -> i32 {\n        return self.value\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var c = Counter::create()\n    c.increment()\n    c.add(10)\n    print(\"Counter value: \")\n    println(c.get())\n    return 0\n}\n```\n",
  "09_pointers_and_memory.md": "# Chapter 9: Pointers, Memory & Casting\n\n## 9.1 Raw Pointers (`*raw T`)\n\nRad provides low-level raw pointers that represent direct hardware memory addresses:\n\n- `*raw T`: A pointer to memory holding values of type `T`.\n- `*raw u8`: A raw byte pointer (analogous to `uint8_t*` or `char*` in C).\n- `*raw *raw u8`: Multi-level raw pointer (used for `argv` arrays and string tables).\n\n---\n\n## 9.2 Pointer Indexing & Dereferencing\n\nPointers in Rad can be indexed directly using bracket syntax (`ptr[index]`), functioning like an unbounded memory view:\n\n```rad\nfn print_bytes(ptr: *raw u8, len: usize) {\n    var i: usize = 0usize\n    while i < len {\n        let b: u8 = ptr[i] // Read byte at offset i\n        print(\"Byte: \")\n        println(b as i32)\n        i = i + 1usize\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Writing Through Pointer Indexing\n```rad\nfn zero_buffer(buf: *raw u8, len: usize) {\n    var i: usize = 0usize\n    while i < len {\n        buf[i] = 0u8 // Write byte at offset i\n        i = i + 1usize\n    }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9.3 Dynamic Memory Allocation (`malloc`, `free`)\n\nBecause Rad programs link against the canonical Musl target sysroot (`libc.a`), the standard heap allocation primitives are direct zero-cost calls:\n\n```rad\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn malloc(size: usize) -> *raw u8;\n    fn free(ptr: *raw u8) -> void;\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    // 1. Allocate 64 bytes on the heap\n    let size: usize = 64usize\n    let buffer: *raw u8 = malloc(size)\n\n    if (buffer as usize) == 0usize {\n        println(\"Memory allocation failed!\")\n        return 1\n    }\n\n    // 2. Write data into the allocated buffer\n    var i: usize = 0usize\n    while i < 10usize {\n        buffer[i] = (65usize + i) as u8 // Write ASCII 'A', 'B', 'C', ...\n        i = i + 1usize\n    }\n    buffer[10usize] = 0u8 // Null-terminate\n\n    // 3. Print buffer as a string slice\n    print(\"Buffer string: \")\n    println(buffer as str)\n\n    // 4. Free allocated memory\n    free(buffer)\n    println(\"Memory freed successfully.\")\n\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nBuffer string: ABCDEFGHIJ\nMemory freed successfully.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9.4 Pointer Arithmetic & Address Offsets\n\nPointer arithmetic in Rad is performed transparently using index offsets (`ptr[offset]`) or by converting the pointer to an integer address with `as usize`, performing byte-level arithmetic, and casting back:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let msg: str = \"Rad Systems Programming\"\n    let p_msg: *raw u8 = msg as *raw u8\n\n    // Skip the first 4 bytes (\"Rad \") by pointer address arithmetic:\n    let addr: usize = (p_msg as usize) + 4usize\n    let p_sub: *raw u8 = addr as *raw u8\n\n    print(\"Original: \")\n    println(msg)\n    print(\"Sub-slice: \")\n    println(p_sub as str)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nOriginal: Rad Systems Programming\nSub-slice: Systems Programming\n```\n\n---\n\n## 9.5 Memory Safety Best Practices\n\n1. **Always Match `malloc` with `free`**: For heap allocations created with `malloc()`, ensure they are freed when no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.\n2. **Prefer Built-In `String` and `Vec`**: In application code, prefer the standard heap collections `String` and `Vec<T>`, which automatically manage their own buffers, dynamic reallocation, and growth.\n3. **Check for Null Pointers**: Validate pointer addresses against zero (`(ptr as usize) == 0usize`) when receiving pointers from OS APIs or C libraries.\n",
  "10_strings_and_collections.md": "# Chapter 10: Strings, Collections & Formatting\n\n## 10.1 The Built-In `String` Type\n\nRad provides a heap-allocated, dynamically resizable `String` type built directly into the compiler's standard library and code generator.\n\n### Creating Strings\n- **`String::new()`**: Creates a new empty dynamic string with initial preallocated heap capacity.\n- **`String::from_str(s: str)`**: Creates a dynamic string initialized with a copy of an existing string literal or slice.\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var s1 = String::new()\n    var s2 = String::from_str(\"Hello, Rad!\")\n\n    println(s2.as_ptr())\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### Mutating Strings\n- **`s.push_str(src: str)`**: Appends a null-terminated string slice to the end of `s`. Automatically reallocates buffer capacity if needed.\n- **`s.push_byte(byte: u8)`**: Appends a single byte (character) to `s`.\n- **`s.len()`**: Returns the current byte length of the string (excluding the null terminator).\n- **`s.as_ptr()`**: Returns a null-terminated raw byte pointer (`*raw u8` / `str`) to the internal buffer, suitable for printing or passing to C APIs.\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var greeting = String::from_str(\"Welcome\")\n    greeting.push_str(\" to the Rad Language\")\n    greeting.push_byte(33u8) // '!'\n\n    print(\"Text: \")\n    println(greeting.as_ptr())\n    print(\"Byte count: \")\n    println(greeting.len())\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nText: Welcome to the Rad Language!\nByte count: 28\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10.2 The Built-In `Vec` Type\n\nRad provides a dynamically resizable heap vector (`Vec<T>`) for sequential collections:\n\n### Creating Vectors\n- **`Vec::new()`**: Allocates a new vector with initial default capacity.\n\n### Vector Operations\n- **`v.push(item: T)`**: Appends an element to the end of the vector, doubling capacity when full.\n- **`v.pop() -> T`**: Removes and returns the last element from the vector.\n- **`v.get(index: usize) -> T`**: Retrieves the element at the specified index.\n- **`v.len`**: Struct field containing the current number of elements in the vector.\n- **`v.cap`**: Struct field containing the allocated capacity.\n- **`v.ptr`**: Raw pointer to the element array (`*raw T`).\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var numbers: Vec<i32> = Vec::new()\n\n    // Append items\n    numbers.push(10)\n    numbers.push(20)\n    numbers.push(30)\n\n    print(\"Vector length: \")\n    println(numbers.len)\n\n    // Access elements via get():\n    print(\"numbers[0]: \")\n    println(numbers.get(0usize))\n    print(\"numbers[1]: \")\n    println(numbers.get(1usize))\n\n    // Pop the last element:\n    let last = numbers.pop()\n    print(\"Popped item: \")\n    println(last)\n    print(\"Remaining count: \")\n    println(numbers.len)\n\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nVector length: 3\nnumbers[0]: 10\nnumbers[1]: 20\nPopped item: 30\nRemaining count: 2\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10.3 Standard Output Printing (`print`, `println`)\n\nRad provides built-in `print()` and `println()` functions that accept strings, integers, and primitive values:\n\n```rad\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let title: str = \"Player Status\"\n    let score: i64 = 4500\n    let alive: bool = true\n\n    println(title)\n    print(\"Score: \")\n    println(score)\n\n    if alive {\n        println(\"Status: ALIVE\")\n    }\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nPlayer Status\nScore: 4500\nStatus: ALIVE\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10.4 Standard C Formatting (`printf`)\n\nFor complex C-style format specifiers (`%08x`, `%.2f`, `%p`), you can call the standard libc `printf` function directly:\n\n```rad\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn printf(fmt: str, ...) -> i32;\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let hex_val: i64 = 255\n    printf(\"Hexadecimal: 0x%04X\\n\", hex_val)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n",
  "11_c_interop.md": "# Chapter 11: C & C++ Interoperability\n\n## 11.1 The Rad C ABI Philosophy\n\nRad does not treat C as an afterthought or an awkward foreign interface. Rad is designed with **mechanical sympathy** for the C ABI:\n\n- Primitive integers (`i32`, `i64`, `u8`, `usize`) directly map to C hardware types (`int32_t`, `int64_t`, `uint8_t`, `size_t`).\n- Pointers (`*raw u8`, `*raw T`) use standard 64-bit machine pointer representation.\n- Functions adhere strictly to the System V AMD64 ABI calling convention on Linux.\n- Executables statically link against the canonical Musl libc (`libc.a`).\n\nAs a result, calling C functions from Rad\u2014or calling Rad functions from C\u2014incurs **zero runtime conversion overhead, zero boxing, and zero marshaling layers**.\n\n---\n\n## 11.2 Declaring Foreign C Functions (`extern \"C\"`)\n\nTo declare C library functions or functions implemented in external C object files, use the `extern \"C\"` block:\n\n```rad\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn puts(s: str) -> i32;\n    fn getenv(name: str) -> str;\n    fn system(cmd: str) -> i32;\n    fn malloc(size: usize) -> *raw u8;\n    fn free(ptr: *raw u8) -> void;\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let user = getenv(\"USER\")\n    if (user as usize) != 0usize {\n        print(\"Current user: \")\n        println(user)\n    }\n\n    // Call libc system() directly:\n    system(\"uname -a\")\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11.3 Struct Memory Layout & C Interoperability\n\nRad structs have standard C memory layout without hidden padding or metadata headers. A Rad struct can be passed directly by pointer to C functions:\n\n### 1. In C Header / Implementation (`matrix.c`):\n```c\n// matrix.c\n#include <stdint.h>\n\nstruct Vector2D {\n    int64_t x;\n    int64_t y;\n};\n\nint64_t vector_dot(const struct Vector2D* a, const struct Vector2D* b) {\n    return (a->x * b->x) + (a->y * b->y);\n}\n```\n\n### 2. In Rad (`main.rad`):\n```rad\n// main.rad\nstruct Vector2D {\n    x: i64,\n    y: i64,\n}\n\nextern \"C\" {\n    fn vector_dot(a: *raw Vector2D, b: *raw Vector2D) -> i64;\n}\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let v1 = Vector2D { x: 3, y: 4 }\n    let v2 = Vector2D { x: 2, y: 5 }\n\n    let dot = vector_dot(&v1 as *raw Vector2D, &v2 as *raw Vector2D)\n    print(\"Vector dot product: \")\n    println(dot)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11.4 Compiling & Linking C Code with Rad\n\nWhen your project includes custom C algorithms, hardware device drivers, or third-party C libraries:\n\n### Step 1: Compile C Object with `rad-clang`\nUse Rad's bundled C compiler:\n```bash\nrad-clang -c matrix.c -o matrix.o\n```\n\n### Step 2: Compile and Link with `radc`\nPass the compiled `.o` file directly to `radc`:\n```bash\nradc main.rad matrix.o -o my_app\n./my_app\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nVector dot product: 26\n```\n\n---\n\n## 11.5 Compiling C++ with `rad-clang++`\n\nRad bundles `rad-clang++` alongside `rad-clang`, complete with bundled C++ standard library headers.\n\nTo compile C++ translation units:\n```bash\nrad-clang++ -c helper.cpp -o helper.o\n```\n\nWhen linking C++ objects that use the standard C++ library (`std::string`, `std::vector`, `std::cout`), pass `-lstdc++` or `-lc++`:\n```bash\nradc main.rad helper.o -o my_cpp_app\n```\n",
  "12_compiler_cli.md": "# Chapter 12: Compiler (`radc`) & Build Deep-Dive\n\n## 12.1 Compiler CLI Flags (`radc`)\n\nThe `radc` binary is the self-hosted Rad compiler. Its command-line interface provides precise control over code generation, optimization, and linking:\n\n```bash\nradc <file1.rad> [file2.rad ...] [options]\n```\n\n### Options Reference:\n\n| Flag | Description | Default |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `-o <path>` | Set output binary path or object path | Auto-named / `a.out` |\n| `-c` | Compile source files to a relocatable object file (`.o`) without linking | Disabled |\n| `-O0` | Disable LLVM optimizations for fastest compile speed | \u2014 |\n| `-O1` | Basic optimizations | \u2014 |\n| `-O2` | Standard production optimization (recommended) | **Default** |\n| `-O3` | Aggressive optimization (auto-vectorization, loop unrolling) | \u2014 |\n| `--emit-llvm`, `--emit-ir` | Emit textual LLVM IR (`.tmp.ll`) without assembling or linking | Disabled |\n| `--check` | Lex and parse all inputs to verify syntax and types without code generation | Disabled |\n| `--target <triple>` | Set compilation target architecture | `x86_64-unknown-linux-musl` |\n| `--sysroot <dir>` | Override canonical target sysroot directory | Autodetected via `$RAD_HOME` |\n| `-V`, `--version` | Display compiler version and build information | \u2014 |\n\n---\n\n## 12.2 Inspecting Generated LLVM IR (`--emit-llvm`)\n\nBecause `radc` directly emits textual LLVM IR, you can inspect the exact low-level SSA representation produced by the compiler:\n\n```bash\nradc main.rad --emit-llvm -o main.ll\n```\n\nOpen `main.ll` to inspect the generated SSA instructions:\n\n```llvm\n; ModuleID = 'radc_compiled'\nsource_filename = \"rad\"\ntarget datalayout = \"e-m:e-p270:32:32-p271:32:32-p272:64:64-i64:64-f80:128-n8:16:32:64-S128\"\ntarget triple = \"x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\"\n\ndeclare ptr @malloc(i64)\ndeclare void @free(ptr)\ndeclare i32 @puts(ptr)\n...\n```\n\nThis is invaluable for systems engineers wanting to verify zero-overhead abstractions, loop vectorization, and memory layouts.\n\n---\n\n## 12.3 Embedded In-Process LLVM & LLD Backend Engine\n\nStarting in Rad 0.2.0, `radc` does not invoke external CLI subprocesses (`rad-llc` or `rad-lld`). Instead, `radc` directly embeds the LLVM and LLD libraries in-process via a C++ backend bridge:\n\n### 1. In-Process LLVM TargetMachine Object Emission\nWhen compiling an executable or object file, `radc`:\n1. Constructs the complete textual LLVM IR in memory.\n2. Invokes LLVM's C API (`LLVMParseIRInContext2`, `LLVMCreateTargetMachine`).\n3. Emits relocatable machine code (`.o`) directly to the build target using LLVM's `LLVMTargetMachineEmitToFile`.\n\n### 2. In-Process LLD ELF Static PIE Linking\nFor final binary linking, `radc` invokes the embedded LLD driver (`lld::elf::link`) in-process:\n- Relocation model: **Static Position-Independent Executable (`static-pie`)**\n- Sysroot assets linked:\n  - Startup objects: `rcrt1.o`, `crti.o`, `crtn.o`\n  - Runtime libraries: `libc.a` (Musl), `libclang_rt.builtins-x86_64.a`\n- Linker flags: `--no-dynamic-linker -z text -z noexecstack`\n\nThis architecture eliminates all process fork/exec overhead, avoids temporary bitcode disk thrashing, and yields instant single-command compilation.\n\n---\n\n## 12.4 Why Position-Independent Executables (Static PIE)?\n\n- **Full ASLR (Address Space Layout Randomization)**: The Linux kernel loads the executable code, stack, and heap at randomized memory addresses, preventing Return-Oriented Programming (ROP) and code injection exploits.\n- **Zero Shared Library Dependencies**: Executables contain their own startup relocator (`rcrt1.o`) and standard C library (`libc.a`). There is no dynamic linker dependency (`no PT_INTERP` / `no /lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2`), allowing the same binary to run effortlessly across Alpine Linux, Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora, Arch Linux, and container roots.\n",
  "13_package_manager.md": "# Chapter 13: Package Manager (`rad`) CLI Reference\n\nThe `rad` executable is the official package manager, project orchestrator, and developer toolchain utility for the Rad language.\n\n## 13.1 Commands Overview\n\n```text\nUsage: rad <command> [options]\n\nCommands:\n  new <name>   Create a new Rad package: `rad new <name> [--lib]`\n  init         Initialize a new Rad package in the current directory\n  build        Compile the current package\n  run          Build and execute the current package\n  test         Run tests for the package\n  check        Analyze package for errors without compiling\n  clean        Remove target build directory\n  doctor       Inspect and verify compiler, embedded backend, and sysroot\n```\n\n---\n\n## 13.2 Command Details\n\n### 1. `rad new <name>`\nCreates a new directory with a complete Rad project skeleton:\n\n```bash\n# Create an application project (src/main.rad)\nrad new my_app\n\n# Create a library project (src/lib.rad)\nrad new my_lib --lib\n```\n\n### 2. `rad init`\nInitializes a new Rad package in the current working directory without creating a new parent directory:\n\n```bash\nmkdir my_project && cd my_project\nrad init\n```\n\n### 3. `rad check`\nChecks all source files in the project for lexical, syntactic, and structural errors using `radc --check`. Does not invoke code generation or linking, making it near-instant:\n\n```bash\nrad check\n```\n\n### 4. `rad build`\nCompiles the project into an executable or library artifact using `radc`'s embedded LLVM and LLD engine:\n\n```bash\n# Debug build (places output in target/debug/main)\nrad build\n\n# Production release build (LLVM -O3, places output in target/release/main)\nrad build --release\n\n# Explicit target override:\nrad build --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\n```\n\n### 5. `rad run`\nBuilds and executes the project's primary binary in one step:\n\n```bash\nrad run\n\n# Run with arguments passed to the application:\nrad run -- --port 8080 --verbose\n```\n\n### 6. `rad test`\nExecutes test functions and verification suites defined in the package:\n\n```bash\nrad test\n```\n\n### 7. `rad clean`\nRemoves the `target/` directory containing intermediate objects and final binaries:\n\n```bash\nrad clean\n```\n\n### 8. `rad doctor`\nVerifies all components of the Rad toolchain, ensuring that `radc` (with its embedded LLVM and LLD backend), standard library sources, and the Musl target sysroot are present and correctly configured:\n\n```bash\nrad doctor\n```\n\nOutput:\n```text\nRad Toolchain Doctor\n====================\n\n  [OK] radc compiler: found (/home/username/.rad/bin/radc)\n  [OK] Embedded LLVM & LLD backend: integrated in-process\n  [OK] Standard library sources: verified at /home/username/.rad/lib/rad\n  [OK] Target sysroot (x86_64-unknown-linux-musl): verified at /home/username/.rad/lib/targets/x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\n\nStatus: Toolchain is healthy and ready for self-contained native compilation.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 13.3 Manifest Specification (`rad.toml`)\n\nEvery Rad package root contains a `rad.toml` manifest file:\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"my_app\"\nversion = \"0.2.0\"\nedition = \"2026\"\n\n[dependencies]\n# Dependencies declaration\n```\n",
  "14_standard_library.md": "# Chapter 14: Standard Library Reference\n\nThe Rad Standard Library is organized into three distinct tiers based on dependency boundaries:\n\n```\nlibrary/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 core/   # Fundamental math, option, result, memory traits (no heap required)\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 alloc/  # Dynamic memory allocation, strings, and collections (heap enabled)\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 std/    # Pure native OS systems: terminal, keyboard, console, time, io, fs\n```\n\n---\n\n## 14.1 Core Library (`library/core`)\n\nThe `core` library contains fundamental language operations that do not require dynamic heap allocation.\n\n### Math Operations (`library/core/src/math.rad`)\n```rad\npub fn min(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64\npub fn max(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64\npub fn abs(x: i64) -> i64\npub fn clamp(val: i64, min_val: i64, max_val: i64) -> i64\n```\n\n### Option Type (`library/core/src/option.rad`)\nRepresents optional values (present or absent):\n```rad\npub struct Option {\n    is_some: bool,\n    value: i64,\n}\n\npub fn some(val: i64) -> Option\npub fn none() -> Option\n```\n\n### Result Type (`library/core/src/result.rad`)\nRepresents either success or error:\n```rad\npub struct Result {\n    is_ok: bool,\n    val: i64,\n    err: i64,\n}\n\npub fn ok(val: i64) -> Result\npub fn err(err_code: i64) -> Result\n```\n\n---\n\n## 14.2 Alloc Library (`library/alloc`)\n\nThe `alloc` library provides dynamically sized types backed by heap allocation:\n\n- **`String`**: Dynamically resizable heap string buffer with UTF-8 byte manipulation, appending, and length queries.\n- **`Vec<T>`**: Contiguous heap array buffer with amortized O(1) growth, pushing, popping, and indexing.\n\n---\n\n## 14.3 Standard Library (`library/std`) \u2014 Pure Native Systems Infrastructure\n\nStarting in Rad 0.2.0, the standard library provides comprehensive native systems modules implemented entirely in pure Rad using direct Linux system-call intrinsics\u2014with **zero dependencies on C, libc, ncurses, or SDL**.\n\n### 1. Terminal Control (`library/std/src/terminal.rad`)\nProvides complete ANSI and POSIX terminal management:\n- **Raw Mode**: Enables raw unbuffered keyboard input via `tcgetattr`/`tcsetattr` kernel ioctls.\n- **Alternate Screen Buffer**: Switches cleanly between primary and alternate terminal screens (`\\x1b[?1049h`).\n- **Cursor Manipulation**: Hide, show, move to `(row, col)`, save and restore cursor position.\n- **Coloring**: Foreground and background styling using 16 standard ANSI colors and 24-bit TrueColor RGB.\n- **Kitty Keyboard Protocol**: Progressively activates full key event detection, including release events (`\\x1b[>3u`).\n\n```rad\nimport std::terminal\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    terminal::enter_alternate_screen()\n    terminal::enable_raw_mode()\n    terminal::hide_cursor()\n\n    terminal::set_fg(terminal::Color::Cyan)\n    terminal::move_cursor(5, 10)\n    print(\"Welcome to Native Rad Terminal UI!\")\n\n    terminal::reset_color()\n    terminal::show_cursor()\n    terminal::disable_raw_mode()\n    terminal::leave_alternate_screen()\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Keyboard & Input Handling (`library/std/src/keyboard.rad`)\nParses non-blocking terminal input into structured `KeyEvent` records:\n- **Key Codes**: Arrow keys (`Up`, `Down`, `Left`, `Right`), `Enter`, `Escape`, `Backspace`, `Tab`, `Space`, and alphanumeric characters.\n- **Release Event Detection**: Full support for Kitty keyboard protocol release codes (`evt.is_release`, `evt.is_key`), preventing typematic keyboard repeat delays in action games.\n- **Modifiers**: Detects `Ctrl`, `Alt`, and `Shift` key combinations.\n\n```rad\nimport std::keyboard\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    var kb = keyboard::Keyboard::new()\n    \n    while true {\n        let evt = kb.poll_event()\n        if evt.has_event {\n            if evt.key == keyboard::KeyCode::Escape {\n                break\n            }\n            if evt.is_key(keyboard::KeyCode::Up) && !evt.is_release {\n                println(\"Moving UP\")\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Console & Double-Buffered Canvas (`library/std/src/console.rad`)\nDouble-buffered terminal canvas for rendering tear-free animations, game frames, and interactive dashboards:\n- **`ConsoleBuffer`**: In-memory character grid with color attributes.\n- **Box Drawing**: Rectangles, borders, centered text, and horizontal progress bars.\n- **Differential Flush**: Minimizes terminal escape emissions by flushing only modified cells.\n\n### 4. Monotonic Time & Timers (`library/std/src/time.rad`)\nHigh-resolution timing implemented via direct Linux `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)` and `nanosleep` kernel system calls:\n\n```rad\nimport std::time\n\nfn main() -> i32 {\n    let t0 = time::now_ns()\n\n    // Precise sub-millisecond sleep\n    time::sleep_ms(16) // ~60 frames per second pacing\n\n    let elapsed_ns = time::now_ns() - t0\n    print(\"Elapsed nanoseconds: \")\n    println(elapsed_ns)\n    return 0\n}\n```\n\n### 5. File System & Process Management\n- **`fs.rad`**: Direct kernel open, read, write, and close operations without libc wrappers.\n- **`process.rad`**: Child process spawning, argument passing, environment inspection, and waitpid exit status queries.\n",
  "15_editor_support.md": "# Chapter 15: Tooling & Editor Support\n\n## 15.1 Visual Studio Code Extension\n\nRad provides a dedicated VS Code extension located in [`editors/vscode-rad`](file:///home/abhinav/Projects/rad/editors/vscode-rad):\n\n### Features:\n- **Syntax Highlighting**: Complete TextMate grammar covering keywords (`fn`, `let`, `var`, `struct`, `if`, `else`, `while`, `return`, `as`, `pub`, `break`, `continue`, `import`, `extern`), primitive types, operators, numbers, and strings.\n- **Bracket Matching & Auto-Closing**: Automatic bracket, parenthesis, and quote pairing.\n- **Comment Toggling**: Single-line (`//`) and block (`/* */`) comment shortcuts.\n- **Code Snippets**: Quick templates for functions, structs, while loops, and `main()`.\n\n### Installation in VS Code:\nInstall the packaged `.vsix` file using the `code` CLI:\n\n```bash\ncode --install-extension editors/vscode-rad/rad-lang-0.2.0.vsix\n```\n\n---\n\n## 15.2 Tree-Sitter Grammar\n\nFor modern editors supporting Tree-Sitter (Neovim, Emacs, Helix, Zed), Rad provides an official Tree-Sitter parser grammar in [`editors/tree-sitter-rad`](file:///home/abhinav/Projects/rad/editors/tree-sitter-rad):\n\n- `grammar.js`: Precise formal syntax specification.\n- `queries/highlights.scm`: Syntax tree highlighting queries.\n- `queries/indents.scm`: Automatic indentation logic.\n- `queries/locals.scm`: Scope and local identifier definition queries.\n\n### Using with Neovim:\nYou can register Rad with `nvim-treesitter` to get instant, accurate AST-based syntax highlighting and indentation.\n"
};
